=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
The fault lies entirely in router0's FRR routing daemon configuration, not in the
mswitch1 trunk as the team suspected. The L2 switching infrastructure is healthy:
mswitch1's br_vlan10 and br_vlan20 bridges have all ports (access and uplink) in
the STP forwarding state with zero dropped packets, and PC4 can successfully reach
its VLAN 10 gateway at 172.16.10.1 on router0, confirming the trunk delivers
packets normally.

The actual break is at the routing layer on router0. Its /etc/frr/ripd.conf file
is empty (0 bytes). Although /etc/frr/daemons has ripd=yes, ripd started with an
empty configuration — no "router rip" stanza and no "network" statements — so it
never joined the RIP domain and never installed any learned routes. Router0's FIB
contains only directly connected subnets and two kernel statics; 192.168.200.0/24
(Location C) and every other remote prefix are completely absent. When Location A
hosts send traffic to Location C, packets arrive at router0 (gateway is reachable
over the functional trunk), but router0 has no forwarding entry and immediately
returns ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" — exactly what the ping tests showed.
The rest of the network (router1, router2) is fully converged on RIP and carries
complete routing tables, confirming the fault is isolated to router0's missing RIP
configuration.

Root cause: /etc/frr/ripd.conf on router0 was left empty, preventing ripd from
activating any RIP instance or network statements at startup.

Fix: Populate /etc/frr/ripd.conf with a valid RIP configuration (e.g., "router rip
/ version 2 / network 10.0.0.0/8 / network 172.16.0.0/12 / network 172.20.0.0/14")
and restart FRR on router0, or apply the equivalent configuration live via vtysh
and write memory. Once router0 participates in RIP it will learn and advertise all
remote prefixes, restoring full connectivity from Location A.
=== END ===
